#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139645 :tutorial/content "To factor a polynomial, the first step is to find the common factor of all the terms and factor it out. Once we've done that, we can try to apply other factoring tricks that we've learned for quadratic expressions.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $2x^3-2x.$\n\nFirst, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $2x.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $2x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n2x^3-2x = 2x(x^2-1)\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the expression $x^2-1$ using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n2x(x^2-1) = 2x(x+1)(x-1)\n$$\n\nSo, the expression $2x^3-2x$ factors into $2x(x+1)(x-1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85714b2f-9623-52ab-aa32-df1827f75877" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3366 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
